#~
Try() -- and '?->', which is Try() on the chain -- recovers from an invalid
object cast, whether or not the failing method happened to be JIT-compiled.

Both halves of #900's fix used to stop at the interpreter's edge, so the SAME
program could recover or abort depending on how many times the failing method
had been called. That is what #925 closed for the cast: the handler stack is
per-thread, so the bridge callback that raises a failing cast can see it; the
callback reports a status instead of exiting; and both backends test that status
and leave through the epilogue, where the interpreter already recovers a guard
stub's status.

THE WARM-UP IS PART OF THE TEST. AsDog is called repeatedly with a cast that
SUCCEEDS so the JIT compiles it, and only then is it given a Cat. Without that,
the method is called once, stays interpreted, and this test would cover the
compiled path only under OBJECK_JIT_THRESHOLD=1 -- which is to say it would look
like coverage it did not have. The method holding the '?->' stays interpreted
either way: TRY_START is in neither backend's whitelist, so the handler always
lives in an interpreted frame, which is the shape the recovery relies on.

The recursion half of #900 lives in try_recovers_depth.obs, which still opts out
of the JIT pass -- compiled frames consume the real C stack, so that one cannot
be reached by this mechanism at all.

Unguarded, a failing cast still prints its message and exits 1; the diagnostic
is suppressed only when a handler is active, so a recovered cast is as silent
here as in interpreted code.
~#

class Animal { New() {} }
class Dog from Animal { New() { Parent(); } }
class Cat from Animal { New() { Parent(); } }

class Holder {
	@animal : Animal;

	New(animal : Animal) {
		@animal := animal;
	}

	# fails when the animal is a Cat
	method : public : AsDog() ~ Dog {
		return @animal->As(Dog);
	}
}

class TryRecoversCast {
	function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
		ok := true;

		# Warm AsDog past the JIT threshold with casts that succeed, so the
		# failing call below runs in COMPILED code in every pass.
		dog_holder := Holder->New(Dog->New());
		warm := true;
		each(i : 500) {
			if(dog_holder?->AsDog() = Nil) {
				warm := false;
			};
		};
		ok := Report("a valid cast returns its object while warming", warm) & ok;

		cat_holder := Holder->New(Cat->New());
		bad_cast := cat_holder?->AsDog();
		ok := Report("invalid cast in a guarded chain yields Nil", bad_cast = Nil) & ok;

		# the program carries on, and the recovered frame did not break the next call
		good := dog_holder?->AsDog();
		ok := Report("a valid cast after recovery returns its object", good <> Nil) & ok;

		if(<>ok) {
			Runtime->Exit(1);
		};
	}

	function : Report(name : String, ok : Bool) ~ Bool {
		if(ok) {
			"PASS: {$name}"->PrintLine();
			return true;
		};

		"FAIL: {$name}"->PrintLine();
		return false;
	}
}
